# .shellcheckrc -- what shellcheck means in THIS tree.
#
# Read by shellcheck itself from the repository root, so `shellcheck <file>`
# typed by hand, `bin/shellcheck-lint.sh`, and CI all get the same answer.
# ONE source, not a flag list retyped into three callers -- the build-
# discipline row about config read from one place is the whole reason this
# file exists rather than an `-e` string inside the guard.
#
# EVERY DISABLE BELOW NAMES ITS EVIDENCE. A suppression without a reason is
# indistinguishable from a suppression nobody re-examined, and this tree has
# already paid for that once: bin/markdown-cost.sh:71 carried
# `# shellcheck disable=SC2254 -- the pattern is meant to glob`, which
# shellcheck cannot parse (it wants `# comment`, not `-- comment`). The
# directive was MALFORMED, so SC2254 was never actually suppressed and two
# parse errors were emitted instead. A suppression that does not suppress is
# worse than none, because it reads as handled.

# SC1091 -- "not following: file not found". 29 hits, every one a `.` of a
# sibling lib resolved at runtime from a path shellcheck cannot evaluate
# statically. Following them needs -x plus a source-path, which makes the
# check depend on cwd; the `# shellcheck source=` directives already in the
# tree are the honest form and they are kept.
disable=SC1091

# SC2016 -- "expressions don't expand in single quotes". 84 hits, and single
# quotes are correct in all of them: awk programs, jq filters, printf format
# strings, and prompt text that must reach an agent verbatim. Enabling it
# would flag the correct spelling of every awk call in the tree.
disable=SC2016

# SC2034 -- "appears unused". 119 hits, dominated by the verb contract:
# VERB_NAME / VERB_SUMMARY / VERB_USAGE / VERB_CAN_SUMMON are set by a verb
# and consumed by lib/verb.sh AFTER it is sourced, which shellcheck cannot
# see across the source boundary. Same shape for CLI_NAME / CLI_SUMMARY /
# CLI_USAGE and bin/lib/*.sh's exported sets.
disable=SC2034

# THREE DISABLES, AND NO MORE. The list above is global -- it silences a code
# in every file, forever, including files nobody has written yet. That is only
# honest for classes that are STRUCTURAL here (a language idiom or a limit of
# static analysis), never for classes that merely happen to have no live bug.
#
# Everything else with hits today goes in bin/shellcheck-lint.ratchet instead.
# The difference is the whole design: a global disable makes the NEXT
# occurrence invisible too, while a baseline records the ones that exist and
# fails on the next one. Two classes that read as "obviously deliberate" are
# baselined rather than disabled for exactly that reason:
#
#   SC2088  tilde-in-quotes, 7 hits -- all human-facing message text
#           (`ok "~/.local/bin exists"`) or ssh_config literals that ssh
#           expands itself. Deliberate HERE. A tilde in a path being handed to
#           `mkdir` would be a real bug, and disabling the code globally is
#           what would hide it.
#   SC2087  unquoted here-doc delimiter, 2 hits -- bin/deploy-drift.sh and
#           bin/selfdev-release-tick.sh send `bash -s` over ssh with `\$uid`
#           escaped for the remote and `$UID_MIN` interpolated locally, on
#           purpose. Quoting EOF would break the local half. A THIRD such
#           heredoc should still have to argue for itself.
#
# NOT disabled and NOT forgiven -- these are the classes that have actually
# cost this estate something, so they stay loud and live in the ratchet where
# they can only go down:
#   SC2164  cd without || exit      -- a test that cd-fails runs somewhere else
#   SC2115  rm -rf "$var/"          -- an empty var makes that `rm -rf /`
#   SC2086  unquoted expansion      -- the classic word-split
#   SC2181  $? instead of `if cmd`  -- the exit-0-no-op shape, in miniature
